    <tok fileIndex="0" linenr="2177" column="1" str="/* Quote from RFC2818 section 3.1 &quot;Server Identity&quot;xx   If a subjectAltName extension of type dNSName is present, that MUSTx   be used as the identity. Otherwise, the (most specific) Common Namex   field in the Subject field of the certificate MUST be used. Althoughx   the use of the Common Name is existing practice, it is deprecated andx   Certification Authorities are encouraged to use the dNSName instead.xx   Matching is performed using the matching rules specified byx   [RFC2459]. If more than one identity of a given type is present inx   the certificate (e.g., more than one dNSName name, a match in any onex   of the set is considered acceptable.) Names may contain the wildcardx   character * which is considered to match any single domain namex   component or component fragment. E.g., *.a.com matches foo.a.com butx   not bar.foo.a.com. f*.com matches foo.com but not bar.com.xx   In some cases, the URI is specified as an IP address rather than ax   hostname. In this case, the iPAddress subjectAltName must be presentx   in the certificate and must exactly match the IP in the URI.xx   This function is now used from ngtcp2 (QUIC) as well.x*/"/>
